<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the Developer ID (direct download) build, signed with the
  hardened runtime. Read by tauri.macos.conf.json; .github/workflows/macos.yml
  checks the signed app carries exactly what is declared here.

  The hardened runtime takes capabilities away; an entitlement gives one back.
  The rule here is one entitlement per thing the app does, and nothing it
  does not. Each absence below is deliberate:

  - com.apple.security.device.camera (PRESENT). The QR pairing screen
    (src/components/QrScanner.svelte) calls getUserMedia for video. Under the
    hardened runtime camera access is refused without this entitlement, and
    Info.macos.plist carries the NSCameraUsageDescription the OS shows.
    Video only: no microphone entitlement, the app records no audio.

  - No com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory.
    Those are for an engine that JITs inside the app process (Electron's V8).
    WKWebView runs JavaScript in Apple's own WebContent process, which carries
    its own entitlements.

  - No com.apple.security.network.client. That key belongs to the App
    Sandbox; a Developer ID app that is not sandboxed reaches the network
    (sync relay, reqwest) without it.

  - No keychain-access-groups. The login keychain needs no entitlement for a
    non-sandboxed app. (As of 0.8.0 Cargo.toml enables no keyring backend
    feature, so the keyring crate does not reach the Keychain at all.)

  - No com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation. sqlcipher and
    openssl are linked statically; the app loads no third-party dylib.

  - No com.apple.security.app-sandbox. That is the Mac App Store build's
    concern, and a separate file when it exists.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
